# runtime
/:memory:.ses
/docs/lm-likelihood-evaluation.md
config/speech.json
config/speech-*.tmp
.speech-release-build/
# what this person has settled -- the reading place per book, the narration
# settings and the theme (lib/prefs.py, TO-DO §4.9): this machine's, not the
# software's, and not any book's either (it is not in bundle.SHAPE)
config/prefs.json
config/prefs.json.tmp
# who may reach THIS computer's Parseh, on which port, with which certificate,
# and the tokens of the devices let in (lib/network.py, TO-DO §1.1, §3.3): the
# same kind of thing, and the tokens are as good as keys
config/network.json
config/network.json.tmp
# what a file's bytes hash to, so that asking what a thing is made of does not
# read the whole shelf twice (lib/offline.py): derived data keyed by absolute
# path, true of this machine's disk alone and rebuilt whenever it is missing
config/digests.json
config/digests.json.tmp
config/digests.json.*.tmp
# and which chapters a recording covers, worked out of the book's own .tex
# and remembered against its mtimes (lib/offline.py, _wheres): the same
# kind of derived, machine-local thing
config/wheres.json
config/wheres.json.tmp
config/wheres.json.*.tmp
# the languages somebody added on this machine (lib/newlang.py writes them,
# lib/languages.py reads them after lib/languages.json): theirs, like the
# settings above, and kept out of the software so an update cannot replace it
config/languages.json
config/languages.json.tmp
# whether this Parseh looks for a new version once a day, and what it last
# found (lib/updater.py): this machine's choice, like the settings above
config/updates.json
config/updates.json.*.tmp
# the LaTeX themes (lib/latexthemes.py, TO-DO §8.39): this machine's, like the
# settings above
config/latex.json
config/latex.json.tmp
# the prompts this person wrote for the chatbots (lib/prompts.py, a0.4.2): the
# person's own words, kept by every update
config/prompts.json
config/prompts.json.tmp
# the drawings LaTeX made of the latex blocks (lib/latexdraw.py): derived, made
# again from their blocks whenever they are missing -- never content
markdown/latex/
# the TeX packages Parseh got for the drawings (lib/texpackages.py): content,
# kept by every update; texmf/README.md stays tracked
texmf/*
!texmf/README.md
serve.log
.serve.pid
.setup-done
.tls/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store
# The environment ./install.sh (and the Windows setup) makes when the machine
# has no conda of its own: micromamba and the ilya-frank packages, under the
# checkout so that removing the folder removes all of it.  Hundreds of
# megabytes, rebuilt from environment.yml, never committed.
.runtime/
# An update from Settings (lib/updater.py): the version waiting to be
# installed, each update's backup of the files it replaced, its journal and
# its report, and the helper that applies it -- this install's own, and in
# no release.
.parseh-update/
# Claude Code's own scratch checkouts of this repository
.claude/worktrees/
# A bundle downloaded from the toolbox and dropped at the top of the checkout
# -- a book, a video, a deck, somebody's test -- is content, not software.
/*.zip
# What `python3 lib/release.py build` writes: a release's zip, its checksum
# and its manifest, made from a commit and made again at will -- never
# committed (and .gitattributes keeps it out of an archive besides).
/dist/
# built by ./build.sh; rebuild, do not commit.
# A book lives at books/<language>/<slug>/; the one-level patterns stay for
# a book from before languages, which lies directly under books/.
books/*/*/main.pdf
books/*/*/main.aux
books/*/*/main.log
books/*/*/main.toc
books/*/*/main.out
books/*/*/frankdraft.*
books/*/*/reader/
books/*/*/.build-key
books/*/*/.reader-key
books/*/main.pdf
books/*/main.aux
books/*/main.log
books/*/main.toc
books/*/main.out
books/*/frankdraft.*
books/*/reader/
books/index.html
# generated from lib/languages.json by make_index.py and at server start
lib/langs.css
books/*/.build-key
books/*/.reader-key
# The HTML guide's compiled pages, html-guide/site/, ARE tracked: GitHub
# Pages publishes the committed pages (html-guide/README.md), so a compile
# (the installers, ./install.sh --guide, the guide's front page) shows up
# here as changes to commit with the Markdown they come from.  vendor/ is
# the snapshot of the studio that `build.py --export` writes into a copy of the
# guide; here the live sources are used, and a stray snapshot is never
# committed.  .site-*/ and .old-site/ are a compile swapping itself in, and
# _site/ what `build.py --pages _site` lays out to be published.
html-guide/engine/vendor/
html-guide/.site-*/
html-guide/.old-site/
_site/
# a narration is hundreds of megabytes and yours: never committed
books/*/*/audio/
books/*/*/*.webm
books/*/*/*.mp3
books/*/*/*.m4a
books/*/audio/
books/*/*.webm
books/*/*.mp3
books/*/*.m4a
audiobook/
# personal stores: the Anki decks, their uploads and builds.
# A deck lives at youtube/anki/<language>/<slug>/; the one-level pattern
# covers both the language folders and a deck from before languages, which
# lies directly under anki/, and the two-level one is spelt out so that a
# pattern narrowed later cannot silently start committing someone's cards.
# notetypes.json and seen.json are collection-level and just as personal.
youtube/anki/*/
youtube/anki/*/*/
youtube/anki/*.json
# the exercise decks, the same kind of thing: a deck lives at
# exercises/<language>/<slug>/ with every answer ever given in its schedule
# files -- personal study state.  Both levels spelt out for the same reason
# as above (and the first also covers exercises/.trash/).  exercises/README.md
# stays tracked; a deck travels by its export, not by git.
exercises/*/
exercises/*/*/
# the clip tray (lib/clips.py): recordings cut from a narration or a film or
# recorded from a YouTube video in the tab, and frames captured from a video,
# waiting to go onto a card -- personal, and copied into whatever card or
# document uses one.  clips/README.md stays tracked.
clips/*
!clips/README.md
books/.trash/
youtube/videos/.trash/
youtube/videos/.waveforms/
youtube/videos/*/.trash/
# A VIDEO'S OWN FILM.  A video that is a file on this machine keeps it as
# media.<ext> beside the transcript, and it is content the way a book's
# narration is content -- hundreds of megabytes of it, which is not what a
# repository is for.  The glosses beside it ARE tracked, so a video's work
# survives; the film travels by the download button instead, which packs it
# (lib/bundle.py, `full`).  audio/ under books/ is ignored for the same
# reason a few lines down.
youtube/videos/*/*/media.*
youtube/videos/*/media.*
# and the original of a sound that had to be made playable (media-orig.<ext>),
# and a film or a sound that was SENT and has not been used yet
youtube/videos/*/*/media-orig.*
youtube/videos/.incoming/
tests/fixtures/videos/*/*/media.*
tests/fixtures/videos/*/media.*
# what the studio builds and remembers, not what it holds: a document under
# library/<language>/<id>/ is content, and content is left to git the way a
# book and a video are -- the examples the first start copies in will show as
# untracked, and nothing here hides them
markdown/library/*/*/build/
markdown/library/*/build/
markdown/library/.seeded*
markdown/library/_prompt.md
# the files the studio has open from elsewhere on the computer (markdown/app/openfile.py): a temporary copy of each,
# deleted when the file is closed
markdown/open/
markdown/app/static/fonts/
markdown/exlex/assets/fonts/
# a font on its way into assets/fonts: renamed into place, or removed
markdown/exlex/assets/.font-*.part
markdown/exlex/out-*/
# the studio's xelatex probe: written and removed at every start
markdown/exlex/.probe.*
# the fixture editions and documents under tests/ build like the real ones
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/main.pdf
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/main.aux
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/main.log
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/main.toc
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/main.out
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/frankdraft.*
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/reader/
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/.build-key
tests/fixtures/books/*/*/.reader-key
tests/fixtures/videos/*/*/.trash/
tests/fixtures/anki/build/

# "old stuff/" is the owner's own shelf: earlier books, videos and notes kept
# beside the toolbox to be read and to be tried against it when something
# changes.  It is content, not software, and it is nobody else's -- so it never
# goes to the remote.  The whole directory, so nothing inside it can be added
# by a stray `git add -A` either.
old stuff/
# and under the name it has since been given, so a rename cannot quietly
# take the protection off it
Parseh-Personal/

# "test for books/" is the owner's own bundles, kept beside the toolbox to
# load back into it when something about that door changes.  They are content
# -- one of them is a 219 MB narration -- and they are nobody else's, so like
# "old stuff/" the whole directory never goes to the remote, and a stray
# `git add -A` cannot take it.
test for books/

# The dictionaries lib/lookup.py reads and lib/getdict.py builds: 3-300 MB
# each, somebody else's work under somebody else's licence, and the toolbox
# ships the doors and none of the content.  A language without one simply has
# no lookup; nothing breaks.
dict/*.db
dict/*.jsonl
# and the half-built file a download writes beside the live one: getdict.py
# and getcorpus.py build to `<name>.part` and rename over the target only when
# the build is whole, so a `.part` is a build in flight or one that died --
# never something to commit.
dict/*.part

# The parallel corpora lib/corpus.py reads and lib/getcorpus.py builds, from
# Tatoeba's own exports: a few megabytes each, somebody else's sentences
# under somebody else's licence.  A pair without one simply offers nothing.
corpus/*.db
corpus/*.db.part
corpus/_dl/

# The translation engine and its models (lib/getmt.py): 5 MB of WebAssembly
# and about 20 MB per language pair, none of it ours.  The engine is
# bergamot-translator under the MPL 2.0 and the models are Mozilla's; both
# are downloaded on request and neither belongs in the repository.
mt/

# Speech to text (lib/getstt.py): the program that runs the models (a pip
# tree of about 400 MB, made under stt/runtime/), the two Whisper models
# (1.6 and 3.1 GB, under stt/models/) and the temporary audio of a job
# (stt/tmp/).  All of it is somebody else's work, downloaded on request and
# made on demand; none of it belongs in the repository.
stt/

# The ARASAAC pictograms (lib/getarasaac.py, a0.4.2): about 160 MB of somebody
# else's pictures (CC BY-NC-SA) and the words that name them, fetched on request
# from api.arasaac.org and static.arasaac.org; none of it belongs in the repository.
arasaac/

# Optional, locally installed character component packs (licences stored in each pack).
components/*.db
components/.build-*/

# TODOs/ holds the working notes of whoever works on the toolbox, none of it
# part of it: TO-DO.md, the list of what is left to do, and the per-round
# TO-DO-*.md beside it; the briefs, PROMPT-*.md (one file per piece of work
# waiting to be handed to a session, written out in full so that nothing has
# to be remembered; the TO-DO item that each one belongs to names its file);
# and todoview.py, the little reader for TO-DO.md (python3 TODOs/todoview.py:
# a browser page on 127.0.0.1 that parses that file and shows it, searchable,
# with its §references as links).
/TODOs/
# They used to sit at the top of the checkout.  These rules stay so that a
# copy written there by habit is still never committed.
/TO-DO.md
/PROMPT-*.md
/todoview.py
# xelatex leaves texput.log where markdown/exlex/envsetup.py probes whether a font is
# registered (a dot-file probe it refuses in nonstop mode): a by-product, never content
texput.log

aligner-models/

# Host-local LLM credentials never belong to source, releases or synced prefs.
config/llm.json
config/llm.json.tmp
config/lm-likelihood.json
config/lm-likelihood.json.tmp
youtube/videos/.pending-transcriptions/
llm-scoring/
